The code statements that lead to the bug are:
fe = (struct ocfs2_dinode *)main_bm_bh->b_data;
BUG_ON(!OCFS2_IS_VALID_DINODE(fe));
Reason:
The bug occurs because ocfs2_group_extend() unconditionally assumes the buffer returned by ocfs2_inode_lock() contains a valid dinode and uses BUG_ON to crash the kernel if the signature does not match. However, JBD2-managed buffers can bypass structural validation, so a crafted filesystem can cause an invalid dinode to reach this code, triggering a kernel panic instead of a graceful error.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (!OCFS2_IS_VALID_DINODE(fe)) {
    ret = ocfs2_error(main_bm_inode->i_sb,
                      "Invalid dinode #%llu\n",
                      (unsigned long long)OCFS2_I(main_bm_inode)->ip_blkno);
    goto out_unlock;
}
Reason:
The fix replaces the BUG_ON with an explicit validation check. If the dinode is invalid, ocfs2_error() is called to log the corruption and return an error code, allowing the resize operation to fail gracefully without crashing the kernel. This properly handles crafted or corrupted filesystems.

